Given the equation $\left(2a+2b-1\right)\left(2a+2b+1\right)=99$, we proceed as follows:

First, we recognize that this is a difference of squares formula in disguise:
\begin{align*}
\left(2a+2b-1\right)\left(2a+2b+1\right) &= 99 \\
\left[\left(2a+2b\right)-1\right]\left[\left(2a+2b\right)+1\right] &= 99 \\
\left(2a+2b\right)^{2}-1^2 &= 99 \\
\left(2a+2b\right)^{2} &= 100
\end{align*}

Next, we take the square root of both sides to solve for $2a+2b$:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{\left(2a+2b\right)^{2}} &= \sqrt{100} \\
2a+2b &= \pm 10
\end{align*}

Dividing both sides by 2 to solve for $a+b$:
\begin{align*}
\frac{2a+2b}{2} &= \frac{\pm 10}{2} \\
a+b &= \pm 5
\end{align*}

Therefore, the value of $a+b$ can be either $5$ or $-5$. Hence, the answer is encapsulated as $\boxed{5 \text{ or } -5}$.

