Given the function $f(x)=|x-1|+|x+1|$.
$(1)$ Find the solution set of the inequality $f(x) \geqslant 3$;
$(2)$ If the inequality $f(x) > a^{2}-x^{2}+2x$ holds for all $x$ in $\mathbb{R}$, find the range of the real number $a$.

s:
Solution:
$(1)$ When $x < -1$, the inequality can be rewritten as $1-x-x-1 \geqslant 3$, $\therefore x \leqslant - \dfrac {3}{2}$;  
When $-1 \leqslant x \leqslant 1$, the inequality can be rewritten as $1-x+x+1 \geqslant 3$, which does not hold;  
When $x > 1$, the inequality can be rewritten as $x-1+x+1 \geqslant 3$, $\therefore x \geqslant \dfrac {3}{2}$,  
$\therefore$ The solution set of the inequality $f(x) \geqslant 3$ is $\boxed{\{x|x \leqslant - \dfrac {3}{2}\} \text{ or } \{x|x \geqslant \dfrac {3}{2}\}}$;  
$(2)$ When $x < -1$, the inequality $f(x) > a^{2}-x^{2}+2x$ can be rewritten as $a^{2} < (x-2)^{2}-4$, $\therefore a^{2} < 5$, $\therefore - \sqrt {5} < a < \sqrt {5}$;  
When $-1 \leqslant x \leqslant 1$, the inequality $f(x) > a^{2}-x^{2}+2x$ can be rewritten as $a^{2} < (x-1)^{2}+1$, $\therefore a^{2} < 1$, $\therefore -1 < a < 1$;  
When $x > 1$, the inequality $f(x) > a^{2}-x^{2}+2x$ can be rewritten as $a^{2} < x^{2}$, $\therefore a^{2} < 1$, $\therefore -1 < a < 1$,  
$\therefore$ The range of the real number $a$ is $\boxed{- \sqrt {5} < a < \sqrt {5}}$.

