       &= 6x + 2f'(1) \text{ (since $f'(1)$ is a constant).}
\end{align*}
Now, let's substitute $x = 1$ into the derivative to solve for $f'(1)$:
\begin{align*}
f'(1) &= 6(1) + 2f'(1) \\
0 &= 6 + 2f'(1) - f'(1) \\
0 &= 6 + f'(1) \\
f'(1) &= -6.
\end{align*}
With the value of $f'(1)$, we can determine $f'(x)$:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 6x + 2(-6) \\
       &= 6x - 12.
\end{align*}
Finally, we find $f'(3)$ by substituting $x = 3$:
\begin{align*}
f'(3) &= 6(3) - 12 \\
       &= 18 - 12 \\
